Aurora 2026-10-25: let Shairport Sync 4.3.7 talk to nqptp 1.2.9 (shared memory interface smi11).

Why: Shairport Sync 4.3.7 was released for nqptp's smi10 interface. nqptp 1.2.9 is smi11: it
inserts one uint64_t, clock_change_offset, between local_to_master_time_offset and
master_clock_start_time in each shm_structure_set, and stamps the region with version 11.
Unpatched, 4.3.7 sees version 11 and stops at startup ("too old for the version of nqptp"),
or reports clock_version_mismatch, so AirPlay 2 never gets a PTP clock.

What: the header now matches nqptp 1.2.9's struct field for field, and NQPTP_SHM_STRUCTURES_VERSION
is 11. ptp-utilities.c gains build-time asserts on the record and region sizes and on every
field offset, with the numbers nqptp 1.2.9's own header gives on LP64 (the nqptp package
asserts the same numbers in its %check). Every use of the struct in 4.3.7 goes through these
field names or sizeof (get_nqptp_data's memcpy/memcmp, ptp_get_clock_info, the mmap length),
so nothing reads the old offsets. 4.3.7 ignores the new field, so the smoother clock hand-off
that nqptp 1.2.9 adds for Shairport Sync's development branch is not used here.

Upstream never tested this pairing: upstream pairs 4.3.x with nqptp smi10 (1.2.4 to 1.2.8),
and smi11 with Shairport Sync's development branch. The control-port protocol
("<smi name> <command> ...") and 4.3.7's startup "T" that makes nqptp 1.2.9 create the region
on demand are unchanged between the two.

--- a/nqptp-shm-structures.h
+++ b/nqptp-shm-structures.h
@@ -22,7 +22,7 @@
 
 #define NQPTP_INTERFACE_NAME "/nqptp"
 
-#define NQPTP_SHM_STRUCTURES_VERSION 10
+#define NQPTP_SHM_STRUCTURES_VERSION 11
 #define NQPTP_CONTROL_PORT 9000
 
 // The control port expects a UDP packet with the first character being a command letter
@@ -59,6 +59,10 @@
   uint64_t master_clock_id;             // the current master clock
   uint64_t local_time;                  // the time when the offset was calculated
   uint64_t local_to_master_time_offset; // add this to the local time to get master clock time
+  uint64_t clock_change_offset;         // smi11 (nqptp 1.2.9): when the clock changes while it
+                                        // is in use, the difference between the old and new
+                                        // offsets. Shairport Sync 4.3.7 does not use it; it is
+                                        // here so the layout matches nqptp 1.2.9 exactly.
   uint64_t master_clock_start_time;     // this is when the master clock became master
 } shm_structure_set;
 
--- a/ptp-utilities.c
+++ b/ptp-utilities.c
@@ -44,6 +44,22 @@
 #include "ptp-utilities.h"
 #include <inttypes.h>
 #include <unistd.h>
+#include <stddef.h>
+
+// The reader and nqptp 1.2.9 (the writer) must agree on this layout byte for byte: the region
+// is memcpy'd and the main and secondary records are memcmp'd. These are nqptp 1.2.9's
+// offsets (five uint64_t fields per record, the uint16_t version padded to 8).
+_Static_assert(NQPTP_SHM_STRUCTURES_VERSION == 11, "nqptp 1.2.9 is smi11");
+_Static_assert(sizeof(shm_structure_set) == 40, "smi11 record size");
+_Static_assert(offsetof(shm_structure_set, master_clock_id) == 0, "smi11 layout");
+_Static_assert(offsetof(shm_structure_set, local_time) == 8, "smi11 layout");
+_Static_assert(offsetof(shm_structure_set, local_to_master_time_offset) == 16, "smi11 layout");
+_Static_assert(offsetof(shm_structure_set, clock_change_offset) == 24, "smi11 layout");
+_Static_assert(offsetof(shm_structure_set, master_clock_start_time) == 32, "smi11 layout");
+_Static_assert(offsetof(struct shm_structure, version) == 0, "smi11 layout");
+_Static_assert(offsetof(struct shm_structure, main) == 8, "smi11 layout");
+_Static_assert(offsetof(struct shm_structure, secondary) == 48, "smi11 layout");
+_Static_assert(sizeof(struct shm_structure) == 88, "smi11 region size");
 
 int shm_fd;
 void *mapped_addr = NULL;
